      <link>https://www.nationalreview.com/corner/nature-misdirects-blame-for-the-jason-arday-tragedy/</link>
      <description>The Jason Arday scandal was a tragedy because it resulted in the death, probably by suicide, of a disturbed man who lied and plagiarized his way to the highest levels of academic celebrity at Cambridge University. Everyone should mourn that outcome.
But too many among the liberal intelligentsia seem...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://i0.wp.com/www.nationalreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/arday.jpg?fit=617%2C360&ssl=1" style="max-width:100%; height:auto;" /></p><div><html><body><div><div class="
          article-content
          article-content--headless
          article-content--teaser
        "><p>The Jason Arday scandal was a tragedy because it resulted in the death, probably by suicide, of a disturbed man who lied and plagiarized his way to the highest levels of academic celebrity at Cambridge University. Everyone should mourn that outcome.</p>
<p>But too many among the liberal intelligentsia seem intent on conjuring the wrong causes for the mess their own attitudes created. Rather than recognizing the potentially dire individual, institutional, and societal consequences that can result from elevating people beyond their actual capabilities, the editors of <em>Nature </em>instead castigate Cambridge for turning on Arday when <a href="https://www.theatlantic.com/culture/2026/08/jason-arday-cambridge-professor-death/688304/" rel="noopener">his fabulism and egregious academic wrongdoing</a> <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-02677-0?utm_source=x&amp;utm_medium=social&amp;utm_campaign=nature&amp;linkId=63526469" rel="noopener"></a> ...</p>
</div></div></body></html></div>]]></content:encoded>
